hat acknowledges the study, clarifies its design, and explains why it supports the source-matters argument rather than undermining it.
01:15:54 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a perfect study to bring up, and it’s one of the most misinterpreted trials in nutrition. You’re talking about the **DIETFITS study** (Gardner et al., *JAMA*, 2018), where 609 overweight adults were randomized to either a “healthy low‑fat” or a “healthy low‑carb” diet. After 12 months, both groups lost about the same amount of weight (5–6 kg), and the researchers’ headline was that there was no significant difference between the two diets. On the surface, that seems like proof that the source or macronutrient ratio doesn’t matter—just pick any diet and lose the same weight. But let’s look at what *actually* happened in that study, because it’s not evidence for “source washes out”; it’s evidence for something much more interesting. ### 1. Both diets were specifically designed to be high‑quality sources This wasn’t a study of “Atkins means all the bacon you want” vs. “Ornish means fat-free Snackwells.” The researchers explicitly told **both** groups: - “Maximize vegetable intake.” - “Minimize added sugar, refined flour, and trans fats.” - “Focus on whole, minimally processed foods.” So the “low‑fat” group wasn’t eating fat‑free cookies; they were eating whole grains, legumes, fruits, and vegetables. The “low‑carb” group wasn’t eating butter‑fried processed meats; they were eating avocados, olive oil, nuts, salmon, and fibrous vegetables. In other words, **both groups improved food quality substantially**, just in different macronutrient patterns. The study compared two high‑quality, whole‑food diets, not a source‑matters vs. source‑doesn’t‑matter comparison. ### 2. The common denominator was eating whole foods to satiety without calorie counting Both groups were told to eat to fullness, not track calories. The key result was that **both groups spontaneously reduced calorie intake by 500–600 calories per day** by simply eating more whole foods. That’s an enormous “source effect”—the satiety and lower energy density of whole foods caused a calorie deficit without deliberate restriction. Had they been told “eat whatever you want as long as it’s low‑fat” or “low‑carb,” they might have chosen processed, low‑satiation versions and eaten more. But the study’s emphasis on real food quality drove the weight loss. So it absolutely does not show source washing out; it shows that when you fix food quality high across the board, you can get similar weight loss with different macro ratios. ### 3. Within each diet, the magnitude of weight loss correlated with food quality improvement The same research group later analyzed *what people actually ate* and found that those who lost the most weight—regardless of assigned diet—ate more vegetables, more whole foods, and fewer processed foods. The “source” of calories mattered significantly for individual success, even if the average weight loss between the two diet arms just happened to be similar. ### 4. They didn’t actually end up with totally different macro ratios By the end, the low‑fat group averaged about 30% fat (not ultra‑low) and the low‑carb group averaged about 30% carbs (not keto‑level). So the diets converged somewhat, reducing the expected metabolic contrast. Yet both still lost the same amount—likely because improved food quality (fiber, protein, phytonutrients) in both conditions overwhelmed any subtle macro‑specific effects. ### 5. What the study *actually* proves The DIETFITS trial is a testament to something I’ve been saying all along: **when you focus on the source—whole, minimally processed foods—and let people eat to satiety, they naturally achieve a calorie deficit and lose weight, regardless of whether they prefer a higher‑fat or higher‑carb pattern.** That’s a massive victory for the “source matters” argument, not a refutation of it. It suggests that the common denominator for successful weight loss isn’t a specific macro ratio but rather the displacement of processed, hyper‑palatable foods with real ones. If source didn’t matter, a study comparing a low‑fat diet of Snackwells and sugary cereals to a low‑carb diet of butter‑fried spam and diet soda would also produce equal and sustained weight loss. But it wouldn’t—and no ethical trial would even run that, because the outcomes would likely be poor on satiety, adherence, and health markers. ### A quick analogy Suppose you compare two car engines: one runs on clean, premium gasoline; the other on a different clean synthetic fuel. Both perform well and get similar mileage. That doesn’t prove fuel quality doesn’t matter—it just shows that both clean fuels work well. If you’d compared dirty, unrefined sludge to clean gasoline, you’d see a huge difference. DIETFITS compared two clean, whole‑food diets; it didn’t test processed junk against whole foods. --- So no, that Stanford study doesn’t prove source washes out. It proves the opposite: that people can be successful on different macronutrient patterns as long as the underlying source is high‑quality, minimally processed, and rich in vegetables and fiber. That’s profoundly important and entirely consistent with the idea that source matters.
