 into ATP—and the signals it sends while doing so—determine whether you’ll be healthy, full, and able to sustain that eating pattern for life.” That’s not a separate issue. That’s the rest of biology that you’re leaving out when you stop the story at ATP. So, to directly address your confusion: - No, I haven’t switched to a purely behavioral/psychological argument. The package effects I’ve been describing (satiety hormones, gut barrier integrity, absorption efficiency, inflammatory signals) are all biological. - Your original point that “all food turns into the same energy” is true in the same way that saying “all vehicles burn fuel” is true. It’s not wrong, but it doesn’t explain why a Formula 1 car and a lawnmower differ in performance, maintenance, and longevity. The “same fuel” story is not the whole physics; the engine and the fuel delivery matter too. - The “totally separate issue” you see is actually biology that I’ve been trying to include from the start, but perhaps I wasn’t explicit enough that I consider it part of the same biological continuum, not a spin-off about willpower. I appreciate you pushing on this. It’s forced me to see where my own framing made it sound like I was retreating to a “just don’t overeat” argument, when my entire point is that the source determines, through biology, whether overeating even happens.
